// fleet domain -- ACTIONS: the world-touching layer of the MemQL Cloud control
// plane (epic memql#3852, task memql#3853).
//
// Per the behavioral-constructs split: logic decides, mutations persist,
// ACTIONS TOUCH THE WORLD, automations orchestrate. Each action below is a
// single external capability call rendered from typed args. None of them calls
// a logic, query, mutation or automation; none touches the graph; none carries
// a reactive trigger. That is what keeps them inside the action-rules validator
// (component/memql/callgraph) rather than being baselined out of it.
//
// EVERY ONE IS `capability script(...)`, selecting an ALLOWLISTED capability
// script by its `script` argument. The runner maps that id to a repo-relative
// path (component/automations/steps.capabilityScriptAllowlist) and execs the
// constant path directly with each rendered arg as its own `--name=value` argv
// element -- never the `bash` interpreter, never a `sh -c` string. So an
// event-derived value can only ever select an allowlisted script, and travels
// as data. That property is why provisioning a tenant from an inbound Stripe
// webhook is not the obviously terrible idea it sounds like.
//
// WHAT AN ACTION MAY NOT DECIDE. The tier -> (profile, dbPreset, ha) resolution
// does NOT happen here and does not happen in the scripts either: it happens in
// logic.memql, reading the tier's own spec row. The capability-script contract's
// "no decisions inside" rule and the action shape point the same way -- an
// action renders arguments it was handed.
//
// DRY-RUN DEFAULTS TO TRUE IN THE SCRIPTS, and every action below therefore
// passes `dryRun` through explicitly. An automation that forgot the argument
// would render a tenant and never apply it, which is a visible no-op; the
// reverse default would have a mis-wired trigger tearing down a customer.

use capabilities.shell.{ script }

/// Render a tenant's kustomize overlay and its ArgoCD Application from the tenant component's
/// templates, and adopt it into ArgoCD. Idempotent: re-running with unchanged inputs rewrites
/// byte-identical files and reports changed=false, which is what makes a redelivered provisioning
/// event harmless.
///
/// `maxLlmCalls` / `maxLlmCostUsd` are REQUIRED, and their requiredness is the launch gate
/// (memql#3854). They render the tenant's own spend ceiling, enforced in-process by the LLM guard
/// with no network and no graph read -- which is what makes "no unbounded-spend path exists" a
/// property of the deployment rather than a hope about our metering being correct. The script
/// refuses a zero, because the guard reads zero as UNLIMITED.
action provisionTenant {
  args {
    tenant            string!
    domain            string!
    profile           string!
    dbPreset          string!
    ha                boolean
    engineTag         string
    dbImageTag        string
    backupDestination string
    maxLlmCalls       string!
    maxLlmCostUsd     string!
    repoUrl           string
    targetRevision    string
    dryRun            boolean
  }
  capability script(
    script:            "fleet.tenantProvision",
    tenant:            args.tenant,
    domain:            args.domain,
    profile:           args.profile,
    dbPreset:          args.dbPreset,
    ha:                args.ha,
    engineTag:         args.engineTag,
    dbImageTag:        args.dbImageTag,
    backupDestination: args.backupDestination,
    maxLlmCalls:       args.maxLlmCalls,
    maxLlmCostUsd:     args.maxLlmCostUsd,
    repoUrl:           args.repoUrl,
    targetRevision:    args.targetRevision,
    dryRun:            args.dryRun
  )
}

/// Scale a tenant to zero, leaving every volume, backup and namespace intact. What a trial expiry,
/// an exhausted grace period and an idle hibernation all reduce to. NOT teardown -- a suspended
/// tenant costs storage and resumes with nothing lost.
action suspendTenant {
  args {
    tenant          string!
    includeDatabase boolean
    dryRun          boolean
  }
  capability script(
    script:          "fleet.tenantSuspend",
    tenant:          args.tenant,
    includeDatabase: args.includeDatabase,
    dryRun:          args.dryRun
  )
}

/// Bring a suspended tenant back up at the counts its overlay declares -- including a tier change
/// made while it was down, because the mesh is restored by handing sync back to ArgoCD rather than
/// by scaling to remembered numbers.
action resumeTenant {
  args {
    tenant      string!
    dbInstances string
    dryRun      boolean
  }
  capability script(
    script:      "fleet.tenantResume",
    tenant:      args.tenant,
    dbInstances: args.dbInstances,
    dryRun:      args.dryRun
  )
}

/// Destroy a tenant after taking a final backup. The only irreversible operation in the fleet: it
/// requires the exact phrase 'teardown <tenant>', and a backup that fails ABORTS the teardown
/// rather than proceeding without one.
action tearDownTenant {
  args {
    tenant     string!
    confirm    string!
    skipBackup boolean
    dryRun     boolean
  }
  capability script(
    script:     "fleet.tenantTeardown",
    tenant:     args.tenant,
    confirm:    args.confirm,
    skipBackup: args.skipBackup,
    dryRun:     args.dryRun
  )
}
